#: src/bgp.c:1652
msgid ""
"run ROUTER bgp as=N [network=PREFIX/LEN ...] [neighbor=NODE:AS[:RELATION] ...]\n"
"               [connected=1] [keepalive=30s] [hold=3×keepalive] [retry=keepalive]\n"
"               [mrai=500ms] [prepend=N] [prepend.NEIGHBOR=N] [policy=1]\n"
"               [rov=1 roa=PREFIX/LEN:AS[:MAXLEN] ...] [transport=6] [enh=0] [id=A.B.C.D]\n"
"run ROUTER bgp announce|withdraw PREFIX/LEN\n"
"  network= and neighbor= can be repeated. RELATION says what the neighbor is for\n"
"  this AS: customer, peer or provider (none: no policy on that session).\n"
"  connected=1 also originates every connected network; `withdraw` silences one.\n"
"  policy=0 ignores the relations. prepend=N lengthens the AS path we export to\n"
"  everybody, prepend.NEIGHBOR=N only towards that neighbor.\n"
"  Nothing checks that an AS owns what it announces: that is the hijack lab;\n"
"  rov=1 with roa= entries rejects announcements with the wrong origin.\n"
"  Sessions run over TCP port 179. keepalive is at least 1 s and hold at least 3 s;\n"
"  retry is the ConnectRetry timer. AS numbers may be four octets long.\n"
"  IPv6: prefixes in network=, roa=, announce and withdraw may be IPv6 ones, and\n"
"  connected=1 also originates the connected IPv6 prefixes. A session carries the\n"
"  families of the networks the two routers share (multiprotocol BGP). It runs over\n"
"  IPv4 when they share an IPv4 network, else over IPv6; transport=6 (or\n"
"  transport.NEIGHBOR=6) prefers IPv6. A neighbor can also be [IPV6-ADDRESS]:AS.\n"
"  Routers that share only IPv6 networks still exchange IPv4 prefixes, with an IPv6\n"
"  next hop (extended next hop encoding, RFC 8950); enh=0 turns that off.\n"
"  Counters: bgp.routes (IPv4) and bgp.routes6 (IPv6)."
msgstr ""

#: src/bgp.c:1674
msgid ""
"The Internet is a network of autonomous systems (AS): networks under one administration, each with its own interior routing. BGP is the protocol they use to tell each other which address blocks (prefixes) they can reach. It is a path-vector protocol: an announcement carries the whole list of ASes it has crossed (the AS path). When a router passes a route on, it prepends its own AS number; a router that finds its own number in a path discards it, which prevents loops without any count-to-infinity. When a route disappears an explicit withdrawal is sent.\n"
"\n"
"Two BGP routers talk over a TCP connection to port 179, so BGP itself needs no acknowledgements or retransmissions: a route is sent once and stays valid until it is withdrawn or the session ends. Either router may open the connection (if both do, the one opened by the router with the higher identifier is kept). Each sends an OPEN with its AS number, identifier and proposed hold time, confirms the other's with a KEEPALIVE, and the session is Established; then the whole table is sent as UPDATE messages, and afterwards only the changes. KEEPALIVEs every third of the hold time prove the neighbor is alive; when the hold timer expires, or the connection breaks, everything learned over the session is withdrawn. Errors are reported with a NOTIFICATION, which also closes the session.\n"
"\n"
"Unlike interior protocols, BGP does not look for the shortest path: it applies policy. Each AS first ranks the routes it hears by local preference, which encodes business relations (a route through a customer earns money, through a peer is free, through a provider costs), and only then by AS-path length. Export follows the same logic (Gao-Rexford rules): routes learned from customers are announced to everyone, routes learned from peers and providers only to customers. The result is that paths are valley-free: they go up through providers, cross at most one peering link, and go down through customers; nobody carries traffic for free between two of its providers.\n"
"\n"
"BGP trusts what it is told. An AS that announces a prefix it does not own attracts the traffic of everyone for whom its path looks better (prefix hijack), and announcing a more specific prefix attracts everyone, because forwarding always follows the longest match. Route origin validation (RPKI) lets routers reject announcements whose origin AS or prefix length does not match a signed authorization.\n"
"\n"
"BGP-4 was designed for IPv4: its UPDATE has fixed fields for IPv4 prefixes and one IPv4 next hop. The multiprotocol extensions (RFC 4760) add two optional attributes, MP_REACH_NLRI and MP_UNREACH_NLRI, that carry the prefixes of another address family together with their own next hop, and a capability in the OPEN with which the two routers agree on the families of the session. For IPv6 the next hop is the neighbor's global address and, when the two routers are on the same link, its link-local one, which is what goes in the forwarding table. Everything else is shared: one session, one AS path, the same decision process and policies for both families. The TCP connection itself may run over either protocol, independently of what it carries.\n"
"\n"
"The same attributes solve a problem of dual-stack backbones: links between routers that carry only IPv6 addresses. An IPv4 prefix cannot be announced over such a link in the classic way, because NEXT_HOP must be an IPv4 address. With the extended next hop encoding (RFC 8950), negotiated by a capability in the OPEN, IPv4 prefixes travel in MP_REACH_NLRI with an IPv6 next hop. The route is installed with that next hop, and the IPv4 packets are still sent as IPv4 frames: only the link-layer address is found with Neighbor Discovery instead of ARP. A router on such a path that has no IPv4 address at all forwards IPv4 perfectly well, but cannot be the source of an ICMP error: traceroute shows it as a silent hop.\n"
"\n"
"Not modelled here: iBGP (there is one router per AS and neighbors are directly connected), MED, communities, aggregation and route-flap damping; each UPDATE carries one prefix."
msgstr ""

