#: src/chord.c:898
msgid ""
"run NODE chord [join=NODE] [bits=8] [stabilize=500ms] [succlist=3]\n"
"run NODE chord put key=K value=V\n"
"run NODE chord get key=K\n"
"run NODE chord lookup key=K          only find the node responsible for K\n"
"  Without join= the node creates a new ring; the others join through any member.\n"
"  succlist=r: a node remembers its next r successors and every pair exists in r copies,\n"
"  on its owner and on the owner's next r - 1 successors (r=1: no fallback, no replica)."
msgstr ""

#: src/chord.c:905
msgid ""
"A distributed hash table stores key-value pairs on many machines with no central directory: any node can find the machine responsible for a key, and nodes may come and go. Chord does it with consistent hashing: nodes and keys are hashed onto the same circle of 2^m identifiers, and a key belongs to its successor, the first node met going clockwise from the key's identifier. When a node joins or leaves, only the keys of one arc of the circle change owner.\n"
"\n"
"Knowing only its successor a node could find any key by walking the ring, in O(N) hops. The finger table makes it logarithmic: entry i points to the successor of id + 2^i, so a node knows its neighbourhood densely and the far side of the ring sparsely. A lookup is forwarded to the closest finger preceding the target, which at least halves the remaining distance: O(log N) hops with O(log N) state per node.\n"
"\n"
"Correctness depends only on successor pointers; fingers are an optimisation. A new node asks any member to look up its own identifier and adopts the answer as its successor. Then every node periodically runs stabilize - ask your successor for its predecessor, adopt it if it sits between the two of you, and notify your successor that you exist - and fix_fingers, which refreshes one finger at a time with a lookup. These background tasks repair the ring after joins without any global coordination.\n"
"\n"
"Failures are handled with a successor list: each node remembers its next r successors, so that when the first one stops answering it falls back on the second, and lookups route around dead fingers. Data survives because every pair exists in r copies, on its owner and on the owner's next r - 1 successors: the node that inherits the arc of a dead one already holds a copy, and whenever its neighbourhood changes a node copies the keys it owns to its new successors, so that r copies exist again before the next failure. With r = 1 there is a single copy and a single successor pointer: every crash loses the keys of the dead node. Two names that hash to the same identifier (likely with few bits) are detected and one of them moves to the next position. Here lookups are recursive (each hop forwards the query, the last one answers the origin directly), liveness is checked with pings every stabilization round, and a node that restarts has lost its memory and joins again as a new one."
msgstr ""

