#: src/crdt.c:580
msgid "CRDTs: replicas that converge without coordination"
msgstr ""

#: src/crdt.c:581
msgid ""
"run NODE crdt peers=n1,n2,n3 [type=gcounter|pncounter|orset|lww] [sync=500ms] [skew=TIME]\n"
"run NODE crdt inc [by=N] | dec [by=N]     counter\n"
"run NODE crdt add X | remove X            observed-remove set\n"
"run NODE crdt set V                       last-writer-wins register\n"
"  Every node needs the same peers list. type selects what the replica hosts and which\n"
"  operations it accepts: gcounter (inc), pncounter (inc, dec), orset (add, remove), lww\n"
"  (set); several types separated by commas host one object of each (type=orset,lww).\n"
"  skew shifts the clock used by the register. crdt.converged is 1 when the last state\n"
"  heard from every peer was identical to the local one."
msgstr ""

#: src/crdt.c:590
msgid ""
"Replicating data on several machines forces a choice when the network splits: refuse updates until the replicas can agree (consistency), or accept them everywhere and reconcile later (availability). Conflict-free replicated data types make the second choice safe by construction: replicas accept updates locally, exchange state in any order, any number of times, and are guaranteed to converge to the same value once they have seen the same updates. This property is strong eventual consistency.\n"
"\n"
"A state-based CRDT is a data structure whose states form a join semilattice: there is a merge operation that is commutative, associative and idempotent, and every update moves the state upwards. Because merging is insensitive to order, duplication and delay, the transport can be as unreliable as gossip: here each replica sends its whole state to one random peer at every round (anti-entropy).\n"
"\n"
"The grow-only counter keeps one total per replica; a replica only increments its own entry, the merge is the element-wise maximum and the value is the sum. A plain shared integer would lose concurrent increments; this one cannot. Two such vectors, one for increments and one for decrements, give the PN-Counter.\n"
"\n"
"Sets are harder because add and remove do not commute. The observed-remove set tags every add with a unique identifier; a remove deletes only the tags its replica has observed. An add concurrent with a remove carries a tag the remover never saw, so the element survives: add wins, and no update is lost. The cost is metadata: removed tags must be remembered as tombstones.\n"
"\n"
"The last-writer-wins register simply keeps the value with the highest (timestamp, replica) pair. It converges too, but by discarding one of two concurrent writes, and which one depends on clocks: a replica with a slow clock loses every race. It is the default of many databases and a classic source of silently lost updates. CRDTs do not replace consensus: they cannot enforce invariants that span replicas, such as a balance that never goes below zero."
msgstr ""

