#: src/dhcp6.c:895
msgid "DHCPv6 server and client: addresses and settings for IPv6 hosts"
msgstr ""

#: src/dhcp6.c:897
msgid ""
"run NODE dhcp6 server [pool=FIRST-LAST] [lease=60s] [dns=ADDR] [rapid=1] [if=eth0]\n"
"  pool: IPv6 addresses to lease (at most 64); without it the server is stateless\n"
"  and only hands out settings. lease is the preferred lifetime (valid = 1.5 x).\n"
"run NODE dhcp6 client [mode=auto|stateful|stateless] [rapid=1] [if=eth0]\n"
"  auto follows the router advertisements: M flag = ask for an address, O flag =\n"
"  ask for the settings only, neither = do nothing (the address comes from SLAAC).\n"
"run NODE dhcp6 release\n"
"  Pair it with `run ROUTER ra managed=1` or `other=1`; the default route always\n"
"  comes from the router advertisement."
msgstr ""

#: src/dhcp6.c:906
msgid ""
"IPv6 hosts can configure themselves from router advertisements alone (SLAAC), so DHCPv6 has a narrower job than DHCP for IPv4, and the router decides whether it is used: the M flag of the advertisement says \"addresses are managed, ask a DHCPv6 server\", the O flag says \"take your address from SLAAC but ask a server for the other settings\", typically the DNS servers. In both cases the default route still comes from the advertisement: DHCPv6 has no router option, and no prefix length either, so which neighbours are on-link is again the router's word.\n"
"\n"
"A client has no broadcast to shout on. It sends from its link-local address to the multicast group ff02::1:2, where servers and relays listen. Stateful exchange: SOLICIT, then ADVERTISE from every willing server, REQUEST to the chosen one, REPLY with the address (with the Rapid Commit option the middle two are skipped). The address lives inside an identity association (IA_NA) identified by an IAID, and carries a preferred and a valid lifetime; the association has two timers, T1 when the client sends RENEW to its server and T2 when it sends REBIND to any server, and when the valid lifetime ends the address must go. RELEASE returns it early. Stateless exchange: INFORMATION-REQUEST and REPLY, no lease at all.\n"
"\n"
"Clients and servers are named by a DUID, an identifier that stays the same on every interface and survives a change of network card better than a MAC address; here it is the simplest kind, built from the link-layer address. Left out: relay agents (the server must be on the client's link), prefix delegation, temporary addresses, CONFIRM and DECLINE, and server preference."
msgstr ""

