#: src/dns.c:1414
msgid ""
"run NODE dns zone=NAME [ttl=60] [negttl=30] RECORD...   authoritative server\n"
"  records, as key=owner:value (owner @ = the zone itself, names are relative\n"
"  to the zone unless they end with a dot, addresses may be node names):\n"
"    a=www:10.0.3.10  aaaa=www:2001:db8::10  cname=web:www  mx=@:mail[:10]  txt=@:hello\n"
"    ns=@:ns1  ns=sub:ns.sub  glue=ns.sub:10.0.4.2  glue6=ns.sub:2001:db8:4::2\n"
"    (ns below the apex = delegation; aaaa and glue6 take an IPv6 address or a node name)\n"
"  the root zone is zone=. ; repeat the command to add records or zones.\n"
"run NODE dns resolver root=ADDR [v6=1] [randomize=1] [entropy=30] [timeout=800ms]   recursive resolver\n"
"  root may be an IPv4 or IPv6 address or a node name (v6=1: its IPv6 address); every\n"
"  server answers over both families, and the resolver prefers IPv6 when it can use it\n"
"  (v6=0: it prefers IPv4)\n"
"  randomize=0: fixed source port 53 and sequential ids (for the poisoning lab)\n"
"  entropy=B: random bits in id (up to 16) and source port (the rest, up to 14)\n"
"run NODE dns flush     empty the resolver cache"
msgstr ""

#: src/dns.c:1428
msgid ""
"The Domain Name System is a distributed database that maps names to records (A: an IPv4 address, AAAA: an IPv6 address, NS: the name servers of a zone, CNAME: an alias, MX: the mail exchanger, TXT: free text). The name space is a tree cut into zones; each zone is served by authoritative servers, and a zone delegates a subtree by publishing NS records for it, plus the addresses of those servers (glue). Nobody knows everything: the root servers only know who serves com, org, it...; those know who serves example.com; and so on.\n"
"\n"
"A host does not walk this tree itself. Its stub resolver sends one query with the Recursion Desired bit to a recursive resolver, which iterates: it asks a root server, gets a referral (no answer, NS records in the authority section, glue in the additional section), asks the server it was referred to, and repeats until an authoritative answer arrives, following CNAME aliases on the way. Every record carries a TTL: the resolver caches it for that long, so the next query for the same name, or for another name in the same zone, skips part or all of the walk. Negative answers (NXDOMAIN, or a name without that record type) are cached too, for the time given by the SOA record of the zone. Short TTLs let a change propagate quickly, long TTLs save queries and hide server outages: that is the trade-off.\n"
"\n"
"Messages have a 12-byte header (identifier, flags, four counters) followed by the question and three record sections; repeated names are compressed with pointers. DNS normally runs on UDP, so a resolver accepts a response when it comes from the address it asked, to the port it used, with the identifier and question it sent: an attacker who can guess those values can forge an answer and poison the cache, which is why resolvers randomize both the identifier and the source port. The records a name has and the transport used to ask are independent: an AAAA record can be fetched over IPv4 and an A record over IPv6, and a dual-stack host asks for both. Here: class IN only, no TCP fallback, EDNS or DNSSEC, and delegations need glue."
msgstr ""

