#: src/gossip.c:949
msgid ""
"Gossip (epidemic) protocols spread information the way a rumor or a virus spreads: at every round each node talks to a few peers chosen at random. Nobody coordinates and nobody knows the whole topology, yet the number of informed nodes roughly doubles each round, so N nodes are reached in O(log N) rounds with O(N log N) messages. Random choice is also what makes the scheme robust: lost messages and crashed nodes only slow it down a little, there is no single point whose failure stops it.\n"
"\n"
"Three styles exist. In push, a node that knows the rumor sends it to random peers: very fast at the start, wasteful at the end, when almost every push hits a node that already knows. In pull, every node periodically asks random peers what is new: slow to start (the few informed nodes are rarely asked) but, once half the nodes know, the uninformed fraction squares at every round. Push-pull combines them and needs only O(N log log N) messages. A pushing node must also decide when to stop: here it loses interest after a number of peers answered that they already knew (the feedback/counter variant of Demers et al.). Stopping early leaves a small residue of nodes that never hear the rumor; this is why practical systems pair rumor mongering with anti-entropy, a periodic exchange of digests that repairs any difference, heals partitions and updates nodes that were down.\n"
"\n"
"SWIM applies the same idea to membership: who is in the group and who has failed. Classic heartbeating to everybody costs O(N²) messages; in SWIM every node probes one random member per period, so the load per node is constant. If the ping is not acknowledged in time the prober does not accuse yet: it asks k other members to ping the target for it (ping-req). Only when direct and indirect probes all fail does the member become suspect, and only if the suspicion survives a timeout is it declared dead. The indirect probe filters out failures of a single path; the suspicion period filters out slow or briefly overloaded nodes.\n"
"\n"
"A suspected node that is alive defends itself: each member has an incarnation number that only the member itself may increment. Hearing that it is suspected at incarnation i, it announces alive at i+1, which overrides the suspicion everywhere; a restarted node rejoins the same way. Membership updates (alive, suspect, dead) are not broadcast: they are piggybacked on the pings and acks already flowing, each a logarithmic number of times, so news spreads epidemically at no extra cost. Detection time is constant in expectation and independent of the group size, and false positives can be traded against it by tuning the period, k and the suspicion timeout. Serf/Consul memberlist, Cassandra and many service meshes use this family of protocols, with refinements such as Lifeguard's adaptive timeouts."
msgstr ""

