"run NODE http server [port=80] [objects=3] [objsize=5000]\n"
"run NODE http get HOST[/PATH] [port=80] [keepalive=1] [embedded=1] [v6=1]\n"
"  The server answers / and /index.html with a page that embeds `objects`\n"
"  images, and /N with N bytes. The client fetches the page and then, with\n"
"  embedded=1, every object it references: over the same connection\n"
"  (keepalive=1) or opening a new connection for each (keepalive=0).\n"
"  HOST is a node name or an address; an IPv6 address ([2001:db8::1] as in a\n"
"  URL), or v6=1 with a name, fetches over IPv6. The server answers both."
msgstr ""

#: src/http.c:566
msgid ""
"HTTP is a request/response protocol carried by a TCP connection. The client sends a request made of text lines: a request line (method, path, version), headers such as Host, and an empty line. The server answers with a status line (200 OK, 404 Not Found, ...), its own headers and the body; Content-Length tells the client where the body ends, because TCP itself only delivers a stream of bytes without message boundaries.\n"
"\n"
"A web page is usually an HTML document plus the objects it references. HTTP/1.0 opened a new TCP connection for every object, paying a three-way handshake and a fresh slow start each time. HTTP/1.1 keeps the connection open (persistent connections), so later requests skip the handshake and inherit an already opened congestion window: compare keepalive=1 and keepalive=0 on a path with some delay and the difference in page load time is exactly those saved round trips. Here requests on a connection are strictly sequential; HTTP/2 multiplexes them and HTTP/3 moves the whole thing onto QUIC to avoid head-of-line blocking."
msgstr ""

