"run NODE lb add SERVER [weight=N]   |   run NODE lb remove SERVER\n"
"  vip defaults to the node's first address; port 0 balances every UDP/TCP port.\n"
"  mode=nat rewrites only the destination: the servers must route their answers\n"
"  back through the balancer. mode=fullnat also rewrites the source.\n"
"  affinity=0 turns the flow table off: every packet is scheduled again.\n"
"  key=src hashes the client address only (same client, same server).\n"
"  check=0 disables health checks (ICMP echo; `fall` misses mark a server down)."
msgstr ""

#: src/lb.c:707
msgid ""
"A layer-4 load balancer owns a virtual IP address (VIP). Clients talk to the VIP; for the first packet of each flow the balancer picks a real server and rewrites the destination address, and for the answers it puts the VIP back as the source, so the client never learns which server it reached. A flow table (client address and port -> server) keeps every later packet of the connection on the same server: without it a TCP connection would break as soon as the choice changed. Health checks probe the servers and remove from the pool those that stop answering.\n"
"\n"
"How to pick the server is the interesting part. Round robin cycles through the pool; the weighted variant gives bigger servers proportionally more; least connections sends the new flow to the server with fewest active ones, which adapts to long-lived or slow connections; power of two choices samples two servers at random and takes the less loaded one, which gets most of the benefit with almost no shared state. Hashing the flow (or the client address) needs no state at all and lets several balancers make the same choice independently, but hash mod N has a flaw: when N changes almost every flow maps to a different server. Consistent hashing places servers (as many virtual nodes) and flows on a ring and assigns each flow to the next server clockwise, so that adding or removing one server moves only about 1/N of the flows. Maglev hashing reaches the same goal with a precomputed lookup table filled from per-server permutations, giving very even load and O(1) lookups. The lab counts how many flows are remapped by each scheme when the pool changes."
msgstr ""

