#: src/mac.c:631
#, c-format
msgid ""
"When many stations share one channel, two transmissions that overlap destroy each other: a collision. A medium access protocol decides who may talk and when, without a central arbiter.\n"
"\n"
"ALOHA does not even try: transmit when you have a frame, and if no acknowledgement comes, wait a random time and repeat. A frame survives only if nobody else starts during the frame time before it or during the frame itself, a vulnerable period of two frame times, which gives a throughput S = G·e^(-2G) and a peak of only 18% at G = 0.5 (G is the attempt rate in frames per frame time). Slotted ALOHA lets stations start only at slot boundaries, halving the vulnerable period: S = G·e^(-G), peak 37% at G = 1.\n"
"\n"
"Carrier sensing (CSMA) listens before talking. It cannot be perfect, because a signal takes time to arrive: two stations that start within one propagation delay of each other both hear silence. The smaller a = propagation delay / frame time, the better CSMA works. Variants differ in what they do when the channel is busy: 1-persistent waits and transmits the instant it is free (and collides with everyone else who waited), non-persistent goes away for a random time, p-persistent transmits with probability p in each small slot.\n"
"\n"
"CSMA/CD (classic Ethernet) also listens while transmitting: on a collision it stops at once, sends a short jam, and retries after a random number of slots drawn from a window that doubles at every collision (binary exponential backoff), so the cost of a collision is a few propagation delays instead of a whole frame. Radios cannot listen while they transmit, so Wi-Fi uses CSMA/CA: wait for the channel to stay idle (DIFS), then count down a random backoff that freezes whenever someone else talks, and rely on an acknowledgement to learn about collisions. Two stations that cannot hear each other (hidden terminals) defeat carrier sensing altogether; a short RTS answered by a CTS from the access point reserves the channel so that only the cheap RTS can collide.\n"
"\n"
"Model and simplifications: time advances in ticks of one propagation delay and every station is one tick away from every other; frames have a fixed length; arrivals are Poisson and each station queues up to 16 frames. The theoretical curves assume infinitely many stations and are shown against the measured attempt rate, so expect agreement in shape, not in the last digit. CSMA/CA slots, DIFS, RTS/CTS and ACK durations are in ticks, not 802.11 timings."
msgstr ""

#: src/main.c:20
msgid ""
"hoplab — a network laboratory in your terminal\n"
"\n"
"Usage:\n"
"  hoplab                         interactive menu: catalog, guide, wizard\n"
"  hoplab run SCENARIO [k=v ...]  simulate every node in this window\n"
"  hoplab windows SCENARIO [...]  one tiled window per node plus a monitor\n"
"  hoplab node SCENARIO NODE[,NODE...]   run only these nodes (other machines\n"
"                                 or windows run the others; links are UDP tunnels)\n"
"  hoplab monitor SCENARIO        global view of a distributed run\n"
"  hoplab plan SCENARIO           print the commands for a distributed run\n"
"  hoplab check [SCENARIO ...]    run headless and verify the `expect` lines\n"
"  hoplab list                    scenarios in the catalog\n"
"  hoplab modules | help MODULE   protocols and tools, and how to use them\n"
"  hoplab guide [TOPIC]           study guide and curriculum\n"
"\n"
"Options:\n"
"  --headless        no interface: print the event log (default when not a terminal)\n"
"  --until TIME      headless run length (default: the scenario's `duration`)\n"
"  --detail 1|2|3    overview, protocol messages, every frame\n"
"  --speed X         time scale (0.1 slow motion ... 100)\n"
"  --seed N          random seed (same seed, same run)\n"
"  --quiet           headless: only the result of the expectations\n"
"  --monitor H[:P]   node mode: report to a monitor\n"
"  --mode M          windows: auto, kitty, tmux, print\n"
"  --nodes a,b,c     windows: the nodes that get a window of their own\n"
"  --hub             node mode: also be the monitor for the other processes\n"
"  --baseport N      first UDP port used by tunnels (monitor: N-1)\n"
"  --lang xx         language (default from HOPLAB_LANG / LANG)\n"
"\n"
"SCENARIO is a file or a catalog name (see `hoplab list`); k=v pairs set the\n"
"scenario parameters.\n"
