#: src/qdisc.c:1079
msgid ""
"link A B qdisc=NAME [queue=N] [parameters]   choose the discipline of a link (both directions)\n"
"run NODE qdisc            show the queues of NODE in its panel\n"
"run NODE qdisc show       log the state of every queue of NODE\n"
"run NODE qdisc list       log the available disciplines\n"
"  droptail  default: arrivals are dropped when the queue holds `queue` packets\n"
"  fifo      droptail that records queue length and waiting time for the graphs\n"
"  red       min=N max=N (packets) maxp=0.1 wq=0.02 gentle=1 ecn=0\n"
"  codel     target=5ms interval=100ms ecn=0\n"
"  pie       target=15ms tupdate=15ms ecn=0\n"
"  fq        quantum=1514 (bytes per round); flows are the IP 5-tuples\n"
"  prio      band 0: DSCP >= 40 (EF: tos=184) and ARP; band 1: other DSCP; band 2: best effort\n"
"  tbf       shape=RATE bucket=BYTES (the link bw still applies underneath)\n"
"  Counters: red.drops red.early red.forced red.marks codel.drops codel.marks pie.drops\n"
"  pie.marks fq.drops prio.drops, on the node that transmits."
msgstr ""

#: src/qdisc.c:1093
msgid ""
"Every output interface of a router has a queue: when packets arrive faster than the link can carry them they wait, and when the buffer is full some must be dropped. The queue discipline decides which packet leaves next (scheduling) and which packets are dropped or marked, and when (queue management). The default, a drop-tail FIFO, is the simplest answer and has two well-known problems. First, with a large buffer a persistent overload keeps the queue full: every packet then waits behind hundreds of others and latency explodes while throughput gains nothing (bufferbloat). Second, it signals congestion only when the buffer overflows, so many flows lose packets at the same moment and back off together (global synchronisation).\n"
"\n"
"Active queue management signals congestion early. RED keeps a moving average of the queue length and drops arrivals with a probability that rises linearly between two thresholds, so flows are told to slow down one at a time before the buffer fills; its weakness is that the right thresholds depend on the link and the traffic. CoDel looks at time instead of length: it measures how long each packet sat in the queue and, when that sojourn time stays above a small target for a whole interval, starts dropping at the head with an increasing frequency (interval divided by the square root of the number of drops) until the delay comes back under the target. It needs no tuning across link speeds. PIE reaches a similar goal with a controller that adjusts a drop probability from the estimated queueing delay and its trend. With ECN the same algorithms can mark packets (CE in the IP header) instead of dropping them: a transport that understands the mark slows down without losing data. None of this helps against a sender that does not react, which is why the labs here that use constant-rate UDP show AQM bounding the delay but not removing the loss.\n"
"\n"
"Scheduling divides the link among flows. Strict priority always serves the highest non-empty band, chosen here by the DSCP field: ideal for a little delay-sensitive traffic such as voice, at the risk of starving the lower bands. Fair queueing gives every flow its own queue and serves them in turn; deficit round robin does it in constant time by granting each flow a quantum of bytes per round and carrying the unused part over, so a flow that sends too much only hurts itself. A token bucket shaper limits a flow to a long-term rate while allowing bursts up to the bucket size: tokens accumulate at the configured rate, a packet leaves only when enough tokens are available, and the excess waits (shaping) instead of being dropped (policing). Modern systems combine the ideas: fq_codel and CAKE run CoDel inside each fair queue."
msgstr ""

