#: src/raft.c:1157
msgid ""
"run NODE raft peers=n1,n2,n3,... [election=1000-2000ms] [heartbeat=300ms] [prevote=1] [first=1]\n"
"  Start it on every member with the same peers list (an odd number is best).\n"
"  first=1 shortens only the very first timeout of that member, so that a lab knows who\n"
"  is elected first; the election timeout must stay well above the heartbeat interval.\n"
"run NODE raft put KEY=VALUE   submit a command (forwarded to the leader)\n"
"run NODE raft get KEY         linearizable read: the leader first confirms, with a\n"
"                              heartbeat round, that a majority still follows it\n"
"run NODE raft get KEY stale=1 read the local copy at once (may be out of date)\n"
"  Crash and restart members with `node N down|up`, partition with `link A B down`."
msgstr ""

#: src/raft.c:1166
msgid ""
"Consensus lets a group of machines behave like one reliable machine: they agree on a single sequence of commands (the replicated log) and each applies it, in order, to its own copy of a state machine. As long as a majority is alive and can communicate, the service keeps working and never returns contradictory answers; when no majority is reachable it stops making progress rather than risk an inconsistency. Raft obtains this with a strong leader: one member at a time accepts commands and tells the others what to store.\n"
"\n"
"Time is divided into terms, numbered consecutively; every message carries the sender's term and whoever sees a higher one adopts it and becomes a follower. A follower that hears nothing from a leader for a randomized election timeout becomes a candidate: it increments the term, votes for itself and asks the others (RequestVote). Each member grants at most one vote per term, and only to a candidate whose log is at least as up-to-date as its own (higher last term, or same term and at least as long). A majority of votes makes a leader; randomized timeouts make split votes rare and short-lived. Because any two majorities intersect, at most one leader exists per term, and the vote restriction guarantees it already holds every committed entry (leader completeness).\n"
"\n"
"The leader appends client commands to its log and replicates them with AppendEntries, which also serves as heartbeat. Each request names the index and term of the entry that precedes the new ones; a follower accepts only if its log contains that entry, otherwise it refuses and the leader retries from further back. By induction, two logs that agree on one (index, term) pair agree on everything before it (log matching). A follower whose log diverges — because it once received entries from a leader that failed before committing them — has the conflicting suffix overwritten.\n"
"\n"
"An entry is committed once the leader of the current term has stored it on a majority; committed entries are never lost and are applied in order by everybody. A subtle rule: a leader never commits entries of earlier terms by counting replicas, only indirectly by committing an entry of its own term after them (hence the no-op appended at the start of each term). Without this rule an entry present on a majority could still be overwritten.\n"
"\n"
"Term, vote and log are written to stable storage before answering, so a member that crashes and restarts cannot vote twice in a term or forget an entry it acknowledged; commit index and state machine are volatile and are rebuilt. A member isolated by a partition keeps timing out and inflating its term; when the partition heals it forces a needless election. The pre-vote extension avoids it: before incrementing the term a would-be candidate asks whether it could win, and members that still hear a leader say no. Real systems add log compaction (snapshots), membership changes through joint or single-server reconfiguration. Reads need care too: a leader cut off from the majority does not know it has been replaced, so answering from its own state could return a value that a newer leader has already overwritten. Here a read is linearizable (ReadIndex): the leader notes its commit index, sends a heartbeat round, and answers only when a majority has acknowledged that round and the state machine has applied up to that index; followers forward reads to the leader. `stale=1` skips the check to show the anomaly. Leases trade the round for an assumption about clocks. etcd, Consul and CockroachDB are built on Raft, while Paxos and its Multi-Paxos variants solve the same problem with a less prescriptive structure."
msgstr ""

