#: src/rip.c:452
msgid "RIP — distance-vector routing (RFC 2453)"
msgstr ""

#: src/rip.c:453
msgid ""
"run ROUTER rip [update=30s] [timeout=6×update] [gc=4×update]\n"
"               [split=1] [poison=0] [triggered=1] [redistribute=0]\n"
"               [phase=TIME] [jitter=1]\n"
"  split=0      disable split horizon (to reproduce count-to-infinity)\n"
"  poison=1     split horizon with poisoned reverse\n"
"  triggered=0  no triggered updates: news travel only with the periodic ones\n"
"  redistribute=1  also advertise static routes\n"
"  phase=T jitter=0  first periodic update at T and no random jitter: makes the\n"
"               order of the updates of different routers repeatable"
msgstr ""

#: src/rip.c:462
msgid ""
"RIP is the classic distance-vector protocol. Every router keeps, for each network, the distance in hops and the neighbor to go through, and every 30 seconds tells all its neighbors the whole table. A router that hears \"I reach X in 3 hops\" concludes that it reaches X in 4 through that neighbor, and keeps the shortest offer (Bellman-Ford, run by the network as a whole). Nobody knows the topology: only distances and directions.\n"
"\n"
"The weakness is bad news. When a network disappears, a router may hear from a neighbor a route that actually passed through itself, believe it, and advertise it back one hop longer: the two bounce the route between them while the metric climbs one step per update until it reaches 16, which means infinity. This is count-to-infinity, and it is why RIP networks cannot be more than 15 hops wide. Split horizon (do not advertise a route on the interface it was learned from), poisoned reverse (advertise it there with metric 16) and triggered updates (send changes immediately instead of waiting for the timer) remove the two-router loop and speed convergence up; loops among three or more routers remain possible. A route not refreshed for 180 seconds times out, is advertised as unreachable for a while (garbage collection) and then forgotten.\n"
"\n"
"Here: RIPv2 messages with the real format on UDP port 520, multicast to 224.0.0.9. Authentication and route tags are not implemented."
msgstr ""

#: src/ripng.c:515 src/ui.c:504
msgid "m"
msgstr ""

#: src/ripng.c:530
msgid "RIPng — distance-vector routing for IPv6 (RFC 2080)"
msgstr ""

#: src/ripng.c:531
msgid ""
"run ROUTER ripng [update=30s] [timeout=6×update] [gc=4×update]\n"
"                 [split=1] [poison=0] [triggered=1] [redistribute=0]\n"
"                 [phase=TIME] [jitter=1]\n"
"  The options are those of `rip`. The lab must use IPv6 (`ipv6 on`, IPv6\n"
"  addresses or `auto addr6`). A router may run rip and ripng side by side:\n"
"  the two protocols share nothing but the algorithm."
msgstr ""

#: src/ripng.c:537
msgid ""
"RIPng is RIP for IPv6: the same distance-vector algorithm, the same 30-second updates, the same infinity of 16 hops, the same count-to-infinity problem and the same remedies (split horizon, poisoned reverse, triggered updates). What changes is what IPv6 changes. An entry carries a 128-bit prefix and its length instead of an address and a mask. Messages travel on UDP port 521 to the link-scope multicast group ff02::9, sent from the router's link-local address with hop limit 255, so that a receiver can tell they come from a neighbor on the same link and were not forwarded. The next hop installed in the routing table is that link-local address: routers on a link find each other without any global addressing plan, and the route survives renumbering.\n"
"\n"
"RIPng is a separate protocol instance, not an extension of RIPv2: a dual-stack router runs both, with two tables that may disagree while one of them is still converging.\n"
"\n"
"Here: the real message format (command, version 1, 20-byte entries, the next-hop entry with metric 0xFF is understood on input). Authentication is left to IPsec by the RFC and is not present; route tags are sent as zero."
msgstr ""

