#: src/tcp.c:2552
msgid "TCP — reliable byte streams, flow and congestion control"
msgstr ""

#: src/tcp.c:2554
msgid ""
"run NODE tcp sink [port=5001] [rwnd=BYTES] [readrate=RATE] [sack=1] [delack=40ms]\n"
"run NODE tcp send DEST [port=5001] [bytes=1M | duration=TIME] [cc=reno|tahoe|newreno|cubic|bbr]\n"
"             [sack=1] [ecn=1] [nagle=1] [delack=40ms] [mss=1460] [iw=10] [sndbuf=256k]\n"
"             [ssthresh=SEGMENTS] [rto_min=200ms] [jitter=TIME] [warmup=TIME]\n"
"             [v6=1] [pmtud=1]\n"
"  sink accepts connections and discards the data; readrate makes it a slow\n"
"  reader (flow control). send transfers `bytes`, or sends for `duration`, and\n"
"  reports time, goodput and the steady-state rate (after the first quarter);\n"
"  when several transfers to the same port end, Jain's fairness index too.\n"
"  sack=0 falls back to NewReno recovery; delack=0 acknowledges every segment;\n"
"  jitter delays the start by a random time up to TIME.\n"
"  DEST may be an IPv6 address, or a node name with v6=1: the transfer then runs\n"
"  over IPv6 (the sink accepts both). pmtud=0 clears DF on IPv4 and lets routers\n"
"  fragment instead of discovering the path MTU.\n"
"  Not implemented: timestamps, tail-loss probes; bbr is\n"
"  version 1 without long-term (policer) sampling; tahoe never uses SACK."
msgstr ""

#: src/tcp.c:2570
msgid ""
"TCP turns the unreliable, unordered packet service of IP into a reliable byte stream between two ports. A connection starts with the three-way handshake (SYN, SYN+ACK, ACK) in which the two ends choose initial sequence numbers and exchange options: the maximum segment size, the window scale, and whether selective acknowledgements are permitted. Every byte has a sequence number; the receiver answers with cumulative acknowledgements (\"I have everything before N\"), delayed so that one ACK covers two segments, and buffers segments that arrive out of order. A segment that is not acknowledged before the retransmission timeout (RTO, computed from the smoothed round-trip time and its variance, doubled at each expiry) is sent again. The connection ends when each side has sent a FIN and seen it acknowledged.\n"
"\n"
"TCP is the same over IPv4 and IPv6: only the pseudo-header that the checksum covers and the room left for data differ (an MSS of 1460 bytes on a 1500-byte MTU over IPv4, 1440 over IPv6, whose header is 20 bytes longer). The MSS the ends announce reflects their own links only, so the sender also discovers the smallest MTU of the path: its IPv4 segments carry the don't-fragment flag, and IPv6 routers never fragment, so a router that cannot forward a segment discards it and reports the MTU of the next hop (ICMP \"fragmentation needed\", ICMPv6 \"packet too big\"). The sender lowers its MSS, cuts the segments in flight that no longer fit and sends them again.\n"
"\n"
"Waiting for the timer is slow, so the sender reads the ACKs for signs of loss. With selective acknowledgements (SACK) every ACK also lists the blocks received beyond the hole; the sender keeps a scoreboard of what is SACKed, declares a segment lost when three segments' worth of data above it have arrived, and retransmits every hole of the window in one round trip, sending whenever its estimate of the data still in the network (the pipe) is below the congestion window. Without SACK it only has duplicate ACKs: three of them trigger a fast retransmit of the first hole, and NewReno repairs the following ones one per round trip, each revealed by a partial ACK.\n"
"\n"
"Two windows limit the sender. The receive window, advertised in every segment, is flow control: it keeps a fast sender from overrunning a slow reader, and when it drops to zero the sender probes periodically until it reopens. The congestion window (cwnd) is the sender's own estimate of what the network can carry. It grows exponentially in slow start up to ssthresh, then linearly in congestion avoidance (additive increase); a loss halves it (multiplicative decrease), giving the classic sawtooth. Tahoe restarts from one segment after every loss; Reno adds fast recovery; CUBIC grows the window as a cubic function of the time since the last loss, which suits fast long-distance paths. With ECN, routers mark packets instead of dropping them and the receiver echoes the mark, so the sender slows down without losing data.\n"
"\n"
"BBR does not treat loss as the signal. From every ACK it computes a delivery-rate sample and keeps two estimates of the path: the bottleneck bandwidth (the largest rate seen in the last ten round trips) and the round-trip propagation time (the smallest RTT seen in the last ten seconds). It paces packets at gain x bandwidth and caps the data in flight at a multiple of bandwidth x RTT. STARTUP doubles the rate every round until the bandwidth stops growing, DRAIN empties the queue that this created, PROBE_BW cruises with an eight-phase gain cycle (1.25 to look for more bandwidth, 0.75 to drain what that left, then six phases at 1), and every ten seconds without a new minimum, PROBE_RTT cuts the window to four segments for 200 ms to measure the RTT again."
msgstr ""

