#: src/twopc.c:795
msgid ""
"run NODE twopc coordinator participants=p1,p2,p3 [timeout=1s]\n"
"run NODE twopc participant coordinator=NODE [vote=yes|no] [timeout=1s]\n"
"run COORD twopc begin [tx=NAME] [three=1]   start a transaction (three=1: 3PC)\n"
"run PART twopc vote=no                      change how a participant votes\n"
"  Crash and restart nodes with `node N down` / `node N up` to see recovery.\n"
"  Counters: twopc.committed, .aborted, .decided, .blocked, .ended (coordinator: decisions\n"
"  acknowledged by all), .took_over (3PC termination), .contradicted (a decision that\n"
"  contradicts one already taken: atomicity broken)."
msgstr ""

#: src/twopc.c:803
msgid ""
"A distributed transaction touches data on several machines and must be atomic: either every machine commits it or none does. No machine may decide alone, because another one might be unable to commit; and once a machine has promised, it cannot change its mind.\n"
"\n"
"Two-phase commit (2PC) gives the decision to a coordinator. Phase 1: it sends PREPARE; each participant makes the changes durable without applying them, writes a READY record to its log and votes YES, or votes NO and aborts. Phase 2: if all voted YES the coordinator writes COMMIT to its log - this single log write is the moment the transaction commits - and tells everybody; otherwise it sends ABORT. Participants acknowledge, and the coordinator repeats the decision until all have.\n"
"\n"
"Logs make the protocol survive crashes. A coordinator that restarts finds either a decision, which it sends again, or none, and then aborts (presumed abort). A participant that restarts in the READY state is in doubt and asks the coordinator.\n"
"\n"
"The weakness is the window between a YES vote and the decision. A participant that has voted YES and hears nothing can neither commit (somebody may have voted NO) nor abort (the coordinator may have committed): it must wait, holding its locks, until the coordinator comes back. 2PC is a blocking protocol.\n"
"\n"
"Three-phase commit (3PC) inserts a PRE-COMMIT round between the votes and the commit, so that no process commits while another is still uncertain. A participant that times out before PRE-COMMIT knows nobody committed and aborts; one that times out after it knows everybody voted YES and commits. This removes blocking only if timeouts are reliable failure detectors, that is in a synchronous network without partitions.\n"
"\n"
"What the participants do when the coordinator is silent is the termination protocol. One of them takes over (here: the lowest in the participant list whose timer fires first), asks all the others for their state and applies four rules: if somebody aborted, abort; if somebody committed, commit; if everybody is still uncertain, nobody can have committed, so abort; if somebody is pre-committed, everybody voted YES, so bring the others to PRE-COMMIT and commit. A coordinator that restarts runs the same inquiry instead of trusting its own log. With a partition the rules break: one side may hold a pre-committed participant and commit while the other, all uncertain, aborts; the counter twopc.contradicted records it when the two sides exchange their decisions. With real networks the general answer is consensus (Paxos, Raft): replicate the coordinator's decision so that it survives the crash of any single machine."
msgstr ""

